(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 50-year-old man with acute pericarditis says the pain keeps him awake because it is worse when he lies down. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Keep the head of the bed elevated","Sleep flat to rest the heart","Sleep prone to splint the chest","Take an extra dose at bedtime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial pain is worse supine and better leaning forward, so keeping the head of the bed elevated is advised, along with completing the full treatment course. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a new moderate pericardial effusion with joint aches and fatigue. She has not traveled and has no recent viral illness. Which test is most reasonable to add to her workup?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody","Prostate-specific antigen","Serum lipase","Hemoglobin A1c"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The effusion workup includes blood counts, chemistry, thyroid-stimulating hormone and troponins, and possibly an antinuclear antibody, for example in a young woman with an effusion, to look for lupus. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a large pericardial effusion is hypotensive and tachycardic. His electrocardiogram shows small QRS complexes with low voltage (small amplitude complexes) whose height alternates from beat to beat. Which electrocardiogram finding is this?","choices":["Electrical alternans","Ventricular bigeminy","Second-degree heart block","Diffuse PR depression"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Electrical alternans, alternating QRS amplitude as the heart swings in the fluid, is fairly specific for tamponade and appears with low voltage and tachycardia. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has needed prednisone for several months for pericarditis that did not respond to first-line therapy. Which complication of her treatment should be specifically anticipated?","choices":["Hypoglycemia","Bradycardia","Hearing loss","Osteoporosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids increase the risk of osteoporosis, a particular concern in older adults on prolonged courses, so bone health must be addressed. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman spent 9 days in the hospital for a large pericardial effusion that needed two procedures. She is now weak and needs help to walk. Besides cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery, which service is most appropriate before she goes home?","choices":["Dermatology","Ophthalmology","Genetics","Rehabilitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Older adults are more likely to need rehabilitation after prolonged illness and procedures, which also carry higher morbidity and mortality at her age. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is readmitted 3 weeks after coronary artery bypass surgery with fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). His sternal wound looks clean. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute graft occlusion","Constrictive pericarditis","Dressler syndrome","Sternal wound infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis weeks after myocardial infarction or post-cardiotomy (cardiac surgery) is Dressler syndrome, an inflammatory reaction to the injured tissue; aspirin plus colchicine treats it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a cold 10 days ago. He now has sharp, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) substernal chest pain that is worse lying flat and eases when he leans forward. A pericardial friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) is heard at the left sternal border, and the electrocardiogram shows widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression and ST depression in aVR. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Viral pleuritis","Acute pericarditis","Anterior myocardial infarction","Acute pulmonary embolism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sharp pleuritic, positional pain after a viral illness, a friction rub and widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression (reciprocal changes in aVR) meet well over two of the four diagnostic criteria for acute pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman had a large effusion drained for tamponade. Afterward her jugular venous pressure remains elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), and she remains fluid overloaded. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Simple pericardial effusion","Incessant pericarditis","Effusive-constrictive pericarditis","Recurrent pericarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Effusive-constrictive pericarditis is underlying constriction with a coexisting effusion, usually with tamponade, in which constriction persists after the effusion is drained. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has fatigue, exertional dyspnea, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), ascites and minimal ankle edema. His liver enzymes are mildly raised. Which condition is the key noncardiac mimic to consider?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism","Cirrhosis","Asthma","Pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis is in the differential of constrictive pericarditis because both cause ascites and fluid overload; an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign points back to the heart. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman taking warfarin for a mechanical valve presents with 2 days of sharp chest pain eased by leaning forward, widespread ST elevation and a small effusion. She is afebrile and her blood pressure is 128/78. Which is the most appropriate disposition?","choices":["Admit her to the hospital","Discharge with clinic follow-up","Discharge after one dose of ibuprofen","Outpatient cardiac imaging next week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients on oral anticoagulants are among the higher-risk groups for acute pericarditis (with fever, subacute course, tamponade signs, large effusion, immunosuppression, trauma, raised troponin) and potentially need hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man who recently moved from a region where tuberculosis is common has effusive pericarditis. Pericardial fluid is positive on acid-fast stain. Which treatment may significantly reduce his progression to constriction?","choices":["Indefinite aspirin alone","Antituberculous therapy","Early pericardiectomy","Long-term loop diuretic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If tuberculosis is the underlying etiology, treatment of the tuberculosis may significantly reduce progression to constrictive pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has taken ibuprofen and colchicine for 8 days for presumed viral pericarditis. She still has a temperature of 38.5 degrees Celsius and pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pain, and a new pericardial effusion has appeared on echocardiogram. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Admit and look for another cause","Continue the same drugs for a month","Stop colchicine and add a diuretic","Start anakinra as a first step"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent fever and pain with a new effusion define treatment failure, and failure to respond within 1 week suggests a cause other than idiopathic or viral; no improvement after 7 days favors hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man began having pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pericardial pain 8 weeks ago. Despite treatment the symptoms have never fully gone away, with no symptom-free interval. Which term best describes his course?","choices":["Recurrent pericarditis","Chronic pericarditis","Effusive-constrictive disease","Incessant pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms persisting beyond 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission define incessant pericarditis; past 3 months the label becomes chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a moderate pericardial effusion was stable yesterday. Today her blood pressure is 96/64, her jugular venous pressure is elevated, and she has new sinus tachycardia at 128. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Increase her loop diuretic","Immediate pericardiocentesis","Rate control with a beta blocker","Repeat echocardiogram next week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In tamponade, sinus tachycardia may indicate significant hemodynamic compromise and is an indication for immediate pericardiocentesis, with fluid support until it is done. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 26-year-old marathon runner is diagnosed with acute viral pericarditis and starts ibuprofen and colchicine. He asks when he can return to training. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid all exercise for the rest of his life","Avoid strenuous activity for at least 1 month","Resume running once the chest pain stops","Run on alternate days during treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Strenuous activity should be avoided until symptoms resolve and biomarkers such as C-reactive protein normalize, at least 1 month after diagnosis, keeping the maximal heart rate under 100. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has heart-failure-like edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. The differential is narrowed to constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy. Which laboratory finding would favor constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Positive blood cultures","A relatively low natriuretic peptide","A markedly high natriuretic peptide","A normal sedimentation rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No B-type natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes constriction, but the level is likely lower in constrictive pericarditis than in restrictive cardiomyopathy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with constrictive pericarditis is scheduled for pericardiectomy next week. He takes a loop diuretic for leg edema. How should the diuretic be handled before surgery?","choices":["Give it intravenously","Add a second diuretic","Use it sparingly","Double the dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If surgery is planned, diuretics should be used sparingly in the preoperative period, even though they relieve the fluid overload of constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, dry skin and a heart rate of 54. Echocardiogram shows a large pericardial effusion without chamber collapse. Which test best addresses the cause her features suggest?","choices":["Tuberculin skin test","Blood cultures","Thyroid function tests","Antinuclear antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism (myxedema) is a metabolic cause of pericardial effusion, and thyroid function tests are part of the effusion workup; her symptoms point straight at it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. His family asks what to expect. Which statement about his long-term outlook is most accurate?","choices":["His age and comorbidities predict a worse outcome","Age does not influence the outcome of surgery","His prognosis is as good as a young patient's","Survival without treatment is usually normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Older age, worse functional class and comorbidities such as renal dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia are independent predictors of poor long-term outcome in constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with tamponade from a malignant effusion has a blood pressure of 76/48 while the team prepares for pericardiocentesis. Which measure best supports her in the meantime?","choices":["Intravenous loop diuretic","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Oral beta blocker now","Intravenous fluid resuscitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous fluid resuscitation increases preload and helps prevent right ventricular collapse; it is a temporizing, stabilizing measure until pericardiocentesis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with frothy urine, leg swelling and a low serum albumin is found to have a moderate pericardial effusion. Which tests best evaluate the suspected cause?","choices":["Thyroid function tests","Lyme disease serology","Antinuclear antibody","Albumin and urine protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Albumin and urine protein screen for nephrotic syndrome as a cause of pericardial effusion, alongside thyroid, rheumatologic, infectious and malignancy evaluation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a moderate-to-large pericardial effusion on echocardiogram with no hemodynamic compromise. Viral, uremic, thyroid and rheumatologic workups are unrevealing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start empiric anakinra","Immediate pericardiectomy","Diagnostic pericardiocentesis","Reassure and discharge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostic pericardiocentesis is considered for a moderate-to-large effusion of unknown etiology, as well as during therapeutic drainage for tamponade and with suspected neoplastic, purulent or tuberculous disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has long-standing constrictive pericarditis with marked edema and ascites despite diuretics. A computed tomography scan shows a thick, heavily calcified pericardium. Which treatment offers definitive relief?","choices":["High-dose colchicine","Heart transplantation","Pericardiectomy","Pericardiocentesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis is often a surgical disease; pericardiectomy, removal of part or most of the pericardium, is the definitive treatment and gives a 5-year survival near 80%. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 3 months ago and has been symptom-free for 6 weeks. His sharp, positional chest pain has now returned with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). C-reactive protein is mildly raised. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Ibuprofen with colchicine for 6 months or more","Ibuprofen with colchicine for only 2 weeks","Prednisone alone for 6 months","Anakinra plus prednisone at once"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is a first recurrence, symptoms returning after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer, treated with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug plus colchicine for 6 months or more. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with typical acute pericarditis after a viral illness has a troponin that is above the reference range. Coronary angiography shows normal arteries. Which process does the troponin elevation most likely reflect?","choices":["Myocardial involvement (myopericarditis)","An unrecognized coronary occlusion","Developing constrictive physiology","A false-positive laboratory result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Troponin may be elevated if the myocardium is also affected, myopericarditis (perimyocarditis); an elevated troponin is also a feature that favors hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man who grew up in a country where tuberculosis is common has fever, night sweats and a moderate pericardial effusion. Fluid is obtained by pericardiocentesis. Which fluid studies best address the suspected cause?","choices":["Gram stain and routine culture only","Antinuclear antibody on the fluid","Acid-fast stain and mycobacterial culture","Cytology for malignant cells alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial fluid is sent for an acid-fast bacilli stain and mycobacterial culture to identify tuberculosis, alongside cell count, Gram stain, cultures and cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man with acute pericarditis feels better after 5 days of ibuprofen and colchicine and plans to stop both medicines now. Which counseling point is most important?","choices":["Switch to prednisone to finish faster","Finish the full course to prevent recurrence","Stopping now is safe since the pain is gone","Colchicine can stop; keep the ibuprofen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patient education stresses adherence to the full treatment course to prevent recurrence, which affects about 15 to 30% of patients, together with activity restriction and red-flag symptoms. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with recurrent pericarditis over several years now has leg edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. Constrictive pericarditis is suspected. Which echocardiogram finding would best support it?","choices":["Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","A dilated, weakly contracting ventricle","Septal bounce toward the left ventricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiographic features of constriction are increased pericardial thickness, abnormal septal motion or bounce toward the left ventricle, pronounced respiratory variation in ventricular filling and moderate biatrial enlargement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is discharged after a large viral pericardial effusion was found without tamponade. He asks how the effusion will be monitored. Which follow-up plan is most appropriate?","choices":["No follow-up once symptoms ease","Serial transthoracic echocardiograms","Monthly electrocardiograms alone","Serial chest radiographs alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All large pericardial effusions should be followed with serial transthoracic echocardiograms, with close clinical follow-up by the specialty matching the cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman develops fever and positional chest pain with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) 2 weeks after a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent. Echocardiography shows a small effusion and her vital signs are stable. Which anti-inflammatory regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Indomethacin plus colchicine","Prednisone plus colchicine","Ibuprofen plus colchicine","Aspirin plus colchicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis after a myocardial infarction (Dressler syndrome) is treated with aspirin as the anti-inflammatory plus colchicine; the other nonsteroidal drugs and glucocorticoids are avoided after infarction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
